\title{Permutation, Grand-Product, and Memory Arguments}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2026-08-18}

\p{
    The arguments that enforce global consistency across a trace, as opposed to row-local
    constraints. [Memory](dhsorens-003U) is the hard case and the usual source of unsoundness.
}

\p{
    Questions this page should answer:
    \ul{
        \li{Permutation and multiset-equality arguments; the grand product and its logUp
        replacement.}
        \li{Offline memory checking: reads and writes as multisets of #{(address, value, timestamp)}
        tuples, and the conditions that make it sound.}
        \li{Address-sorted versus timestamp-sorted formulations, and what each costs.}
        \li{Read-write memory in the folding setting.}
        \li{The constraint that is always forgotten: monotonicity of timestamps, and what a missing
        range check on it permits.}
    }
}

\p{
    Sources: [Nebula: efficient read-write memory](https://eprint.iacr.org/2024/1605), ArkLib's
    \code{ProofSystem/ConstraintSystem/MemoryChecking.lean}.
}
